SECTION 3: IMPACT PROJECTION — HORIZON SCAN FRAMEWORK (2026-2031)
Projected impact analysis using the HORIZON Scan across AgriPride's three primary stakeholder groups over a five-year implementation horizon.

Stakeholder
Positive Outcomes
Unintended Consequences
Mitigation Strategies
Stakeholder 1:Smalhlolder Farmers
30-40% increase in seasonal yield accuracy through AI-powered pest detection and contextual crop advisories in local languages. Improved market timing via real-time price intelligence reduces post-harvest losses. Digital financial histories generated by the platform improve SACCO loan eligibility.
Over-reliance on AI advice may erode indigenous agricultural knowledge passed through generations. Farmers who cannot access the platform due to connectivity gaps may be further marginalised relative to those who can.
Integrate community elder advisory sessions into platform design. Develop offline-capable SMS/USSD fallback. Implement PRIDE Loop human review for high-stakes recommendations such as harvest timing and pesticide application.
Stakeholder 2:Local Communities
AI-driven market intelligence reduces middlemen exploitation, increasing farm-gate prices by an estimated 15-25%. Community-level aggregated weather and crop data informs regional food security planning. Digital literacy improves as platform adoption scales.
Data aggregation could inadvertently profile community-level poverty or food insecurity, creating vulnerability to commercial exploitation by third-party data brokers. Displacement of local agronomy extension workers whose roles may be automated.
Implement OASIS Sovereignty-First protocol: all community data stored on African-continent servers under Kenya DPA 2019 governance. Retrain extension workers as AgriPride community AI liaisons rather than eliminating their roles.
Stakeholder 3:Environment and Biodiversity
AI-optimised irrigation recommendations reduce water consumption by an estimated 20% on participating farms. Precision pest detection reduces blanket pesticide application, lowering soil and water contamination. Crop diversity recommendations support polyculture practices that preserve soil health.
Scaled AI-driven monoculture optimisation could inadvertently reduce crop biodiversity if yield-maximisation models favour single high-yield varieties. Increased data infrastructure energy consumption during drought periods.
HORIZON Non-Human Impact clause: embed biodiversity index as a mandatory parameter in all crop advisory algorithms. Source platform energy from solar-powered data centres. Partner with Kenya Forestry Research Institute for biodiversity-aligned AI training data.


SECTION 4: PERSONAL REFLECTION

Before this course, I understood artificial intelligence primarily as a technical capability: a system that processes inputs and generates outputs. What I did not fully appreciate was the degree to which AI is a mirror. It reflects the quality of the questions we ask, the biases embedded in the data we feed it, and the values, or lack thereof, of the people who deploy it. That understanding has fundamentally changed how I approach every AI interaction.

Prompt engineering taught me that precision is not a luxury; it is an ethical obligation. The AIM and MAP frameworks revealed that a vague prompt is not just inefficient, it can be harmful. When a smallholder farmer in rural Kenya receives AI-generated crop advice built on a poorly structured query, the downstream consequence is not a mediocre document. It can be a failed harvest and a family that does not eat. Learning to engineer prompts with audience, intent, method, and grounded sources changed how I see language itself as a form of design.

AI ethics, through the ETHOS, TRACK, and OASIS frameworks, gave me something I did not expect: discomfort. The TRACK framework in particular forced me to ask whose voices are absent from the datasets powering the systems I might build. The OASIS Protocol reframed data not as an asset to be collected but as a covenant to be honoured. I will not build, deploy, or recommend an AI system without first asking who might this harm, and whose consent have I actually obtained.

Agent orchestration through the RANK and TRAIL frameworks showed me that autonomous AI systems are not inherently dangerous, but undisciplined ones are. The architecture of responsibility matters: clear authority limits, immutable audit trails, and human kill switches are not bureaucratic friction. They are the difference between a tool that serves farmers and one that harms them at scale.

In my future career, I intend to carry these frameworks not as academic checklists but as professional reflexes. Every system I build will have an ethics audit before deployment. Every AI interaction I design will be grounded in the real constraints of its intended user, not an imagined ideal. Africa does not need AI that works in a Silicon Valley demo. It needs AI that works in a Kano dry season, on a Nokia feature phone, in Hausa, for a farmer who cannot afford to be wrong. That is the standard I will hold myself to.

